WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.580
This is not CGI. It’s neither a&nbsp;
Sci-Fi video game, nor a movie.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:17.760
This is the town of Vorkuta, Russia and it’s all&nbsp;
too real for the 70,000 people who call it home.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.220
Virtually everything about Vorkuta&nbsp;
screams “Don’t live here.”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.760
starting with the extreme&nbsp;
difficulty of getting there.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:33.660
Nearly a hundred miles north of the Arctic&nbsp;
Circle, it’s the easternmost town in Europe.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:41.520
With no roads in or out, the only ways to&nbsp;
or from Vorkuta are a 40-hour train ride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.460
or one of the two to three weekly flights…&nbsp;
assuming they’re unimpeded by blizzards.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.100
Because of this, those who move away are&nbsp;
liable to just leave behind their cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.920
and apartments with everything in them — which&nbsp;
is how you get apocalyptic scenes like these.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.580
During the winter, temperatures can&nbsp;
drop as low as -58 degrees Fahrenheit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.200
The sun appears for just an hour&nbsp;
on the average December day.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:17.040
Then, after enduring this for 8 long months,&nbsp;
residents are rewarded with a brief, 70-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.560
intermission when the snow melts, revealing the&nbsp;
city’s bright, Soviet-era buildings underneath.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:30.540
Clearly, mother nature would prefer Vorkuta&nbsp;
remain a vast, untouched reindeer sanctuary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.860
So, why does the city exist at all?&nbsp;
What kind of person would move here?

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.100
Initially, the kind who didn’t&nbsp;
have a choice — prisoners.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.160
Chosen precisely for its isolation,&nbsp;
the town was once home to one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.300
largest and most notorious of the&nbsp;
Soviet Union’s forced labor camps.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.500
Later, after Stalin’s death&nbsp;
and the end of the Gulag,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.280
Moscow invested billions of&nbsp;
rubles transforming distant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.840
arctic settlements like Vorkuta into&nbsp;
shining, triumphant symbols of the empire.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:15.420
Conquering nature was no less a political as a&nbsp;
practical project. Like Mao’s obsession with dams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.000
Vorkuta and places like it were irrefutable&nbsp;
physical testaments to the power of Socialism.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.000
These towns — some, though not&nbsp;
all in Siberia — were usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.680
designed around a single industry.&nbsp;
Sometimes even a single factory.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.420
In Vorkuta, that industry was coal mining.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.320
Further south, another one produces asbestos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:46.200
In fact, the town is named after the known&nbsp;
carcinogen, which it still mines to this day.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.480
In total, there are about 300 Russian ‘monotowns’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:58.080
of which Vorkuta is neither the furthest&nbsp;
north, most remote, nor even the coldest.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:05.220
Take, for example, Norilsk, a nickel mine&nbsp;
masquerading as a city of 200,000 people.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.060
Entry is strictly off-limits to foreigners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.500
which may be a good thing, considering it’s&nbsp;
one of the world’s most polluted cities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:22.320
Its hazy, brown air and toxic, discolored water&nbsp;
make Vorkuta, by comparison, look like Disneyland.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.220
Despite this, thousands packed their bags&nbsp;
and cheerfully moved to places like Norilsk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:34.200
and Vorkuta in the 70s and 80s thanks&nbsp;
to the Soviet system of social welfare.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:41.040
Although the retirement age was set very&nbsp;
generously at just 55 for women and 60 for men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.680
you could retire even earlier and collect an even&nbsp;
larger pension if you met certain conditions.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:54.180
…Conditions like working in a mine, or, better&nbsp;
yet, working in a mine… in the middle of nowhere.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.620
That placed Vorkuta right in the&nbsp;
bulls-eye of the Venn Diagram.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.220
Young Russians could move to the arctic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:07.140
do a few years of hard labor, and then&nbsp;
retire early down on the Crimean beach.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.440
But all that came to an&nbsp;
end on Christmas Day, 1991.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.680
The collapse of the Soviet Union meant the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:21.300
collapse of the entire economic model&nbsp;
on which these monotowns were based.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:26.100
Suddenly, these mines, factories,&nbsp;
and power plants were thrown out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.880
to the wolves, forced to fend for&nbsp;
themselves on the global market.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:36.600
After a lifetime of artificial state&nbsp;
support, how could they possibly compete?

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:41.880
So, what happened next was as&nbsp;
predictable as it was tragic.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.020
Bankruptcies led to layoffs. Layoffs led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.460
to poverty. Poverty to crime.&nbsp;
And crime to mass migration.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:57.240
A quarter of the Vorkuta fled in the&nbsp;
four years between 1998 and 2002.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:04.320
Meanwhile, the 1998 financial crisis had reduced&nbsp;
the value of Russian pensions by nearly 50%.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.580
In short: seniors were on&nbsp;
the verge of financial ruin.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:16.380
And what little social welfare remained&nbsp;
was often inconsistent or ineffective.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.980
Retired teachers might be entitled to free bus&nbsp;
rides where public transit no longer existed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:28.740
These but not those veterans might be&nbsp;
granted free phone service… etcetera.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:34.800
This was the Russia that Vladimir Putin&nbsp;
inherited at the turn of the millennium.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:40.140
The entire social welfare system&nbsp;
was in dire need of an overhaul.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.660
And his first attempt to fix it was&nbsp;
called the “monetization of benefits”.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:52.860
Rather than maintain 230 different complex,&nbsp;
outdated categories of benefits — the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:57.600
was — why not just give people cash&nbsp;
and let them decide what to do with it?

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:03.360
In truth, it was a fairly modest proposal&nbsp;
with entirely reasonable intentions.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:10.080
But, the government failed to communicate&nbsp;
its plan effectively. What’s more, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.360
Kremlin delegated implementation to individual&nbsp;
regions, leading to geographic disparities.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:23.220
Russians — everywhere from downtown Saint&nbsp;
Petersburg to rural Siberia — were. furious.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.180
Thousands of protestors descended on the streets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.320
shouting “Down with Putin!” and&nbsp;
waving nostalgic Soviet flags.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:37.140
In their eyes, this was not a mere&nbsp;
change in policy. It was nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:41.520
less than a giant middle finger to their&nbsp;
years of hard labor and national service.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:48.060
These coal miners traveled all the way from&nbsp;
Vorkuta to protest in the streets of Moscow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.320
That’s like swimming from&nbsp;
Guam to the White House lawn.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:56.640
For Putin, the message was loud and clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:02.700
don’t touch pensions — or anything&nbsp;
resembling pensions — with a ten-foot pole.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:08.460
Pensioners became a critical base of&nbsp;
support for his party, “United Russia”.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:15.960
In 2005, on the subject of raising the retirement&nbsp;
age, Putin declared unequivocally on television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:19.500
quote, “this decision will not&nbsp;
be taken while I am president.”

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.900
He later called pensions the Soviet&nbsp;
Union’s greatest achievement.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.360
And after seeing how the issue of social&nbsp;
welfare inspired political interest even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.400
in Siberian coal miners, his government&nbsp;
paid particular attention to these remote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:42.660
decaying monotowns, home to&nbsp;
a large number of retirees.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:48.720
The Kremlin was so concerned that it tasked&nbsp;
the Federal Protective Service — Russia’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:53.400
equivalent to the Secret Service — with&nbsp;
conducting, quote, “sociological surveys”.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:59.100
These undercover agents identified&nbsp;
54 monotowns as having the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:03.480
risk of unrest, with another 104 not far behind.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:08.820
Thus, while other ex-Communist&nbsp;
countries like Poland and Hungary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:13.500
encouraged the residents of their “rust&nbsp;
belt” towns to slowly migrate toward jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:19.980
Russia saw them as delicate kindling, waiting&nbsp;
for the tiniest spark to ignite a wildfire.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:24.600
Less risky to simply keep these&nbsp;
dying regions on life support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:31.020
handing out just enough busy work to ensure&nbsp;
scarce time for any… “extracurricular activities”.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:36.000
These factories may not produce&nbsp;
anything tangible of value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.840
but what they do produce&nbsp;
are votes for United Russia.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.900
High employment plus rising&nbsp;
pensions equals harmony.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:52.200
The first way it achieves this harmony&nbsp;
is with a wildly bloated bureaucracy.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:59.100
In 2011, nearly 20% of the entire national&nbsp;
labor force was employed by the state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:03.540
And that’s not counting state-owned&nbsp;
companies — another 12%.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:10.980
In fact, the pension fund itself employs&nbsp;
over 100,000 people — 60% more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:15.360
its American counterpart — despite&nbsp;
serving a population half as large.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:18.600
You know there’s a problem when the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:22.440
government looks like an efficient,&nbsp;
well-oiled machine by comparison.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:29.460
Secondly, the Kremlin “collaborates” with —&nbsp;
or, more accurately, threatens — businesses.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:34.140
Oligarchs may be given an&nbsp;
incredibly long leash in Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:38.760
but one red line they definitely don’t&nbsp;
dare cross are excessive layoffs.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:44.040
Shut down an unprofitable factory in&nbsp;
a politically sensitive area and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.640
might suddenly develop a dangerous&nbsp;
habit of falling out of windows.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:53.040
When times are tough, oligarchs wisely&nbsp;
try just about anything under the sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.700
before resorting to lay-offs,&nbsp;
including deferring paychecks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:01.500
slowing down recruitment, and giving&nbsp;
workers unplanned, extended holidays.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:08.040
Finally, monotowns are directly&nbsp;
subsidized. In 2010 alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:13.080
$846 million U.S. Dollars&nbsp;
were allocated to 27 towns.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.360
That’s how the Kremlin keeps the&nbsp;
baseline dissatisfaction low.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:25.920
But, Putin also sprinkles in some sweet carrots,&nbsp;
particularly before an important election.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:32.340
As you can see, pensions have increased&nbsp;
substantially since the year 2000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:36.180
They more than doubled between 2012 and ’22.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:45.900
Now, Russian pensions have never been large. The&nbsp;
average amount is only $222 US Dollars a month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:51.000
But remember, this is a country where&nbsp;
the mean annual income is about $8,000.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:11:01.080
For 93% of its 46 million pensioners, this is&nbsp;
the only thing keeping them from extreme poverty.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:07.260
To call these retirees “single-issue&nbsp;
voters” is a massive understatement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:13.140
Either they receive these pensions&nbsp;
or they starve. It’s that simple.

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:18.720
And given the sheer size of this&nbsp;
voting block — one-third of the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:23.400
country(!) — Putin’s extraordinarily&nbsp;
high approval should be no surprise.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:28.440
Over the course of his career, Putin&nbsp;
perfected this high employment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:31.380
rising pension social harmony formula.

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:35.640
But this incredible success was deceiving.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:41.700
Beneath this bandaid, the infection&nbsp;
was getting worse. A lot worse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:46.320
And to see why, we need to take a brief step back.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:54.060
In their simplest form, the way pensions work&nbsp;
is that one group pays in to a giant pool of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:58.020
money and another group withdraws&nbsp;
out of that same pool of money.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:04.260
In other words, the whole concept&nbsp;
requires a balance of payers and payees.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:10.200
This is what that balance looked like&nbsp;
before the Soviet Union collapsed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:18.120
The 1991 population broken down by age,&nbsp;
from young at the bottom to old on top.

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:26.340
Better yet, let’s ignore the kids, combine&nbsp;
everyone aged 20-54 into one “contributor” group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:29.580
and anyone older into a single “recipient” group.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:37.380
Take it all in and now compare that&nbsp;
1991 balance to this 2021 balance.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:42.540
As you can see, the Soviet Union&nbsp;
was playing welfare on “easy mode”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:44.820
with much fewer recipients.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:50.160
Immediately after its fall, the&nbsp;
number of new births collapsed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:56.460
And for every one of the last 32 years&nbsp;
since, Russia has gotten considerably older,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:04.020
tilting pensions off-balance, with 44% more&nbsp;
recipients and slightly fewer contributors.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:12.480
The writing has been on the wall for three decades&nbsp;
— the math simply doesn’t add up. And there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:19.020
no doubt Putin knew this. But to peel back the&nbsp;
band-aid and confront the root cause would mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:24.480
risking a larger, potentially regime-threatening&nbsp;
version of what happened back in 2004.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:33.480
So, he waited. And waited. And waited. Until&nbsp;
the inevitable could be delayed no longer.

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:39.480
To regain balance, the “recipient” group&nbsp;
had to shrink and the “contributor” group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:45.780
had to grow. Which meant Putin had to&nbsp;
do what he vowed never to do 13 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:49.560
earlier on television — raise the retirement age.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:53.400
This time, however, he had a plan.

00:13:54.360 --> 00:14:00.120
On June 14th, 2018, while Russian soccer&nbsp;
fans were busy cheering their defeat of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:05.100
Saudi Arabia on the opening day of&nbsp;
the World Cup, Putin made his move.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:13.320
The retirement age was quietly increased from&nbsp;
60 to 65 for men and from 55 to 63 for women.

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:19.620
With this one change, the Russian retirement&nbsp;
age went from one of the world’s most generous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:24.240
unchanged since its establishment&nbsp;
by Joseph Stalin in the early 1900s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:26.580
to one of the world’s least generous.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:32.700
To make matters worse, these changes were&nbsp;
implemented much faster than in most countries.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:37.620
The United States, for example, is&nbsp;
very slowly raising its retirement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:41.880
age by just 2 years in total&nbsp;
over the course of two decades.

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:47.160
Needless to say, this… was not well received.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:52.500
This is Putin’s popularity since 1999 and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:55.740
15% drop in approval is when&nbsp;
the announcement was made.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:03.540
In days, Putin lost most of the surge in support&nbsp;
he gained from the annexation of Crimea in 2014.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:11.340
This was, in fact, the largest short-term collapse&nbsp;
in approval he’s ever seen — before or since.

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:17.400
And by January, the public’s level&nbsp;
of trust in Putin fell to just 33%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:21.540
with a full 50% preferring the&nbsp;
resignation of his cabinet.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:25.740
The backlash was so intense that&nbsp;
his administration eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:31.080
lowered the retirement age again,&nbsp;
this time from 63 to 60 for women.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:36.240
Though this concession failed to address&nbsp;
one of the most common complaints:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:40.260
a huge number of Russians will never see a dime.

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:46.380
Earlier, we saw this population pyramid&nbsp;
dividing the country into age groups.

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:50.280
But this graph obscures a massive imbalance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:54.660
To see it, we have to split&nbsp;
each age cohort by gender.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:16:01.740
In case it’s still unclear, let’s show only&nbsp;
the excess men or women for each age group.

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:04.620
Notice the pattern?

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:11.280
Down here, men very slightly outnumber&nbsp;
women. Here, between ages 30-34,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:17.580
the two groups are roughly equal. And then&nbsp;
from there, women start to rapidly eclipse men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:23.160
By the time you reach age 70, there&nbsp;
are three women for every one man.

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:29.460
The simple, unfortunate reality is&nbsp;
that Russian men don’t live very long.

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:36.360
Poor healthcare, physically arduous labor, plus&nbsp;
off-the-charts alcohol consumption will do that.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:41.460
To be clear, its life expectancy&nbsp;
has increased substantially in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:47.220
recent years — credit where credit is due&nbsp;
— but that comes after decades of decline.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:55.440
You heard that right. Between 1960 and 94, the&nbsp;
average Russian lost three years of their life.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:59.760
But that’s both men and women. The male&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:05.100
life expectancy is dramatically&nbsp;
lower — a mere 66 years today.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:11.520
And even that’s just a national&nbsp;
average! In poor, rural regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:14.760
the male life expectancy is much, much lower.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:21.660
Remember Norilsk from earlier? Its&nbsp;
life expectancy is 59 and almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:25.260
certainly lower for men, most&nbsp;
of whom work in nickel mines.

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:30.840
That leaves its average resident&nbsp;
with a retirement of… -6 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:35.400
Even the average Russian&nbsp;
male can only expect a year.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:42.060
So, you can probably see why the&nbsp;
2018 change wasn’t met with glee.

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:46.800
Now, I won’t bore you with the&nbsp;
implications of Russia's invasion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:51.060
of Ukraine, the pandemic which overwhelmed&nbsp;
its already underfunded healthcare system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:54.540
or the Western sanctions on&nbsp;
all manner of its exports.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:58.320
…But suffice to say: whatever demographic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:03.420
fiscal problems the country had five&nbsp;
years ago, things are much worse now.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:09.120
The beautiful thing about demographics is&nbsp;
that they allow you to peer into the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:16.320
There are fewer Russian teenagers today, so there&nbsp;
will be fewer pension fund contributors tomorrow.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:22.740
But the devastating thing about demographics&nbsp;
is that, for the most part, they’re fixed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:28.260
Today’s teenagers are 13-year-old&nbsp;
ripples of actions past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:32.460
Unless Putin invents a time machine&nbsp;
or immigrants flock to a country whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:36.840
passport is becoming less powerful by&nbsp;
